<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheonmin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ch'ŏnmin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The cheonmin, the &quot;base&quot; or lowborn people, were the lowest class of Korean society under the Goryeo (918–1392) and Joseon (1392–1897) dynasties, set apart in law from the free &quot;good people&quot; (yangin). Their status was largely hereditary and tied to work the upper classes thought unclean: butchers, shamans, shoemakers, metalworkers, jailers, prostitutes and performers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisaeng">gisaeng</a> entertainers were cheonmin, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobi">nobi</a>, the enslaved servants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangban">yangban</a> and the royal family, were taken from among them. They were barred from government service and the civil service examinations and lived segregated from the rest of society.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…them were the jungin, hereditary specialists such as interpreters, physicians and clerks; the commoners (sangmin), mostly farmers who bore taxes, labour and military service; and the lowborn (<b>cheonmin</b>), above all slaves (nobi), who in the early centuries may have made up a third or more of the population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In law the state divided its subjects into the free “good people” (yangin) and the “base people” (<b>cheonmin</b>), but in practice four status groups formed.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
